  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   - Central Idea: Develop a hybrid optimization strategy combining evolutionary search with gradient-based refinement, preserving translation invariance properties. The approach leverages directional gradient information to guide each phase, accelerating convergence towards globally optimal constructuringlemsadeadb\n   - Bottleneck Addressed: Previous local optimization methods typically converge to suboptimal regions, while binary approaches struggle with sum constraints. The hybrid approach explores broad regions while maintaining tight constraints and utilizes gradient information for directed search.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - Representation: Candidate solutions represented as NumPy arrays `h` of length `n_points` with `0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1` and `sum(h) = n_points/2`\n   - Objective: `C5(h) = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx = max_k corr[k]*(2.0/n_points)`, where `corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")`\n   - Constraints: `0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1` for all `i` and `sum(h) = n_points/2`\n   - Algorithm:\n     1. Phase 1: Global search using gradient-informed crossover with primal-dual adjustment\n     2. Phase 2: Local refinement with directional gradient projection and repair\n     3. Phase 3: Final polish with accelerated simplex search\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   - Startup: Initialize with parent solution if `initial_h_values` available\n   - Phase 1 (Explore):\n     1. Compute direction: `dir_idx, grad = worst_shift_and_grad(h)`\n     2. Crossover: Create `h_new = mass_transfer_move(h, grad, alpha=budget_alpha, target_sum)`\n     3. Repair: Adjust `h_new` to enforce sum constraint (`enforce_sum`)\n   - Phase 2 (Exploit):\n     1. Compute century gradient\n     2. Perform directional adjustment\n     3. Apply probabilistic perturbations\n   - Phase 3 (Polish):\n     1. Volume search with decreasing budget\n     2. Final simplex optimization\n   - Repair Operations:\n     - `enforce_sum(h, rng)`\n     - `clip_to_bounds(h)`\n     - `project_to_feasible(h, grad)`\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - `budget_alpha` values:\n     - Initial exploration: `0.1`\n     - Mid-budget: `0.02`\n     - Finalexploration: `0.004`\n   - Timemarker checks every `300s` of wall clock time\n   - Fallback mechanism triggers when:\n     - Budget exceeds `85%` time\n     - Worst shift exceeds margin\n   - Significant variations:\n     - `variation_binary`: Binary implementation\n     - `variation_hybrid`: Current document\n     - `variation_slsqp`: SLSQP-specialized approach\n\n5. Computational Budget and Robustness\n   - Estimated procedure count:\n     - Phase 1: `400`\n     - Phase 2: `200`\n     - Phase 3: `100`\n   - Time check:\n     - Every `300s` global time\n     - Budget threshold: `98%` utilization\n   - Failure handling:\n     - Null budget: Fall back to recent solution\n     - Constraint violations: Repair via `enforce_sum`\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Final solution check:\n     - `0 <= h[i] <= 1` for all `i`\n     - `abs(sum(h) - target) <= 1e-5 * n_points`\n     - Objective finite (`compute_c5` returns valid value)\n   - Contract specifications:\n     - `n_points` shape preserved\n     - Gradient computation performed\n     - Translation invariance maintained in evaluation\n   - Runtime interface:\n     - `compute_c5(h)`\n     - `worst_shift_and_grad(h)`\n     - `mass_transfer_move(h, grad, alpha, target)`\n     - `enforce_sum(h, rng)`\n     - Python interface: (0 <= x <= 1) for input/output",
